<first_data>
Sure, I'd be happy to help you create an end-to-end solution for a hotel search with an autocomplete feature. Here's a high-level overview of how you can approach each part:

1. Backend filtering function and API endpoint (Node.js):

* Create a Node.js server using a framework like Express.js.
* Implement a filtering function that takes a search query as input and returns a list of matching hotel names. You can use a database like MongoDB or PostgreSQL to store hotel data and perform the search. Here's an example using MongoDB and Mongoose:

```javascript
const Hotel = require('./models/hotel');

app.get('/api/hotels/search', async (req, res) => {
 const searchQuery = req.query.q;
 const hotels = await Hotel.find({ name: { $regex: searchQuery, $options: 'i' } }, 'name').limit(10);
  res.json(hotels);
});
```

* Set up an API endpoint that accepts a search query and returns the filtered hotel names in JSON format.

2. Front-end reusable component to plug and play (JavaScript, HTML, CSS):

* Create a reusable autocomplete component using JavaScript, HTML, and CSS. You can use a library like React or Vue.js to make it easier to create a reusable component. Here's an example using React and the `downshift` library:

```jsx
import Downshift from 'downshift';

const Autocomplete = ({ onSelect, options }) => {
  return (
    <Downshift onSelect={onSelect}>
      {({ getInputProps, getItemProps, isOpen, inputValue, highlightedIndex }) => (
        <div>
          <input {...getInputProps({ placeholder: 'Search hotels...' })} />
          {isOpen && (
            <ul>
              {options
                .filter(option => option.name.toLowerCase().includes(inputValue.toLowerCase()))
                .map((option, index) => (
                  <li
                    {...getItemProps({
                      key: option.name,
                      index,
                      item: option,
                      style: {
                        backgroundColor: highlightedIndex === index ? 'lightgray' : 'white',
                        cursor: 'pointer',
                      },
                    })}
                  >
                    {option.name}
                  </li>
                ))}
            </ul>
          )}
        </div>
      )}
    </Downshift>
  );
};
```

* Use the autocomplete component in your hotel search page and make an API call to the backend to fetch hotel names based on the user's input.

3. System design for autocomplete service:

* To ensure fast and efficient autocomplete suggestions, consider implementing a caching layer using a tool like Redis. This will help reduce the number of database queries and improve the overall performance of the autocomplete service.
* Implement rate limiting and throttling to prevent abuse and ensure the stability of the service.
* Consider using a load balancer to distribute traffic across multiple instances of the backend service and improve scalability.
* Monitor the performance of the autocomplete service and make adjustments as needed to ensure a smooth user experience.
</first_data>

<second_data>
Sure! Here's a simple example of a backend filtering function and API endpoint using Node.js and Express:

1. Install the required dependencies:
```bash
npm init -y
npm install express body-parser
```

2. Create a file named `app.js` and add the following code:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const bodyParser = require('body-parser');

const app = express();
app.use(bodyParser.json());

// Sample hotel data
const hotels = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Hotel A' },
  { id: 2, name: 'Hotel B' },
  { id: 3, name: 'Hotel C' },
  { id: 4, name: 'Hotel D' },
  { id: 5, name: 'Hotel E' },
];

// Backend filtering function
const filterHotels = (hotelName) => {
  return hotels.filter(hotel => hotel.name.toLowerCase().includes(hotelName.toLowerCase()));
};

// API endpoint
app.get('/api/hotels', (req, res) => {
  const hotelName = req.query.name;
  const filteredHotels = filterHotels(hotelName);
  res.json(filteredHotels);
});

// Start the server
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`));
```

3. Run the server:
```bash
node app.js
```

Now, let's create a simple front-end component for the autocomplete feature:

1. Create an `index.html` file with the following content:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Hotel Autocomplete</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
  <div class="container">
    <input type="text" id="hotel-search" placeholder="Search hotel...">
    <ul id="hotel-results"></ul>
  </div>
  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

2. Create a `style.css` file with the following content:

```css
body {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

.container {
  max-width: 600px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

input[type="text"] {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 10px;
  margin-bottom: 10px;
}

ul {
  list-style: none;
  padding: 0;
}

li {
  background-color: #f2f2f2;
  margin-bottom: 5px;
  padding: 10px;
  cursor: pointer;
}
```

3. Create a `script.js` file with the following content:

```javascript
const searchInput = document.getElementById('hotel-search');
const resultsList = document.getElementById('hotel-results');

searchInput.addEventListener('input', () => {
  const hotelName = searchInput.value;
  fetch(`/api/hotels?name=${encodeURIComponent(hotelName)}`)
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(hotels => {
      resultsList.innerHTML = '';
      hotels.forEach(hotel => {
        const li = document.createElement('li');
        li.textContent = hotel.name;
        li.addEventListener('click', () => {
          searchInput.value = hotel.name;
          resultsList.innerHTML = '';
        });
        resultsList.appendChild(li);
      });
    });
});
```

Now, you have a simple autocomplete system for searching hotels by name. The backend filtering function and API endpoint are implemented in Node.js, and the front-end component is implemented using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.
</second_data>

